\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Transpose signs and signature-dependent reality}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Van Proeyen uses a unitary matrix #{C_{\mathrm{VP}}} and signs
#{\epsilon_{\mathrm{VP}},\eta_{\mathrm{VP}}} satisfying
##{\begin{aligned}
C_{\mathrm{VP}}^{T}&=-\epsilon_{\mathrm{VP}}C_{\mathrm{VP}},\\
\gamma_a^T&=-\eta_{\mathrm{VP}} C_{\mathrm{VP}}\gamma_a C_{\mathrm{VP}}^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
His Table 1 lists the transpose signs
\citet{§3.1, (3.11), p. 18 and Table 1, p. 19, v7}{vanproeyen1999tools}.
For Euclidean Hermitian generators in an orthonormal basis
#{\gamma_a^T=\bar\gamma_a}. The corresponding antiunitary
is #{\bar C_{\mathrm{VP}}\kappa_0}, with #{\kappa_0} coordinate
conjugation. Its generator sign is #{\eta=-\eta_{\mathrm{VP}}} and
its square is #{\bar C_{\mathrm{VP}}C_{\mathrm{VP}}
=-\epsilon_{\mathrm{VP}}\bbI}. With these translations the
simple-module entries agree with \ref{fdip-017O}.}

\p{Van Proeyen separately asks when a spinor reality condition,
or a symplectic reality condition on pairs, is compatible
with the signature and with a choice of Weyl chirality
\citet{§3.2, (3.19)-(3.20), p. 20, (3.25), (3.27)-(3.28),
p. 21 and Table 2, p. 22, v7}{vanproeyen1999tools}.
The finite substitution in \ref{fdip-017U} records the
effect of multiplying the spatial generators by #{\ui}.
A signature-dependent physical spinor condition carries
the representation and reality hypotheses of that comparison.}
